  "method_card": {
   "steps": [
    "Identify the data type (categorical, discrete, continuous, bivariate)",
    "Choose the correct chart type for the data",
    "For reading charts: check axes, scale and labels carefully",
    "For pie charts: use angle/360 × total to find frequency"
   ],
   "title": "Representing Data",
   "content": "<p><strong>Bar charts:</strong> Bars represent frequencies. Heights are proportional. Use for categorical or discrete data.</p><p><strong>Pie charts:</strong> Angle per category = \\(\\frac{\\text{frequency}}{\\text{total}} \\times 360°\\). To read: \\(\\frac{\\text{angle}}{360} \\times \\text{total}\\).</p><p><strong>Scatter graphs:</strong> Plot bivariate data. <strong>Positive correlation</strong> — both increase. <strong>Negative correlation</strong> — one increases, other decreases. <strong>No correlation</strong> — no pattern. The <strong>line of best fit (LOBF)</strong> passes through \\((\\bar{x}, \\bar{y})\\). <strong>Interpolation</strong> (within range) is reliable; <strong>extrapolation</strong> (outside range) is not.</p>",
   "example": "<p><strong>A pie chart represents 120 people. The 'Bus' sector is 90°. How many travel by bus?</strong></p><p>\\(\\frac{90}{360} \\times 120 = 30\\) people</p>"
  },
